      "source_text_sha256": "57f28ff5c992e366c65459cad50c30c6b5e6a5009a834170fb660b9bc8b35658",
      "lines": [
        "ImageNet: Necessitated",
        "New Breed of Hardware",
        "• ImageNet’s (inconveniently) large size: ~130 GB",
        "• Typical HW in 2010 meant deep learning needed to shift away from local machines",
        "• RAM: 2-4 GB (stored less than 5% of ImageNet)",
        "• Consequence: MUST stream to disk and so bottleneck shifts to disk I/O",
        "• Storage: 160 - 500 GB (26% to 81% consumed by ImageNet)",
        "• Consequence: inconvenient local storage normalized a shift to external hard drives and",
        "institutional servers",
        "• Compute efficiency: high-end research machines had dual quad-core CPUs;",
        "researchers mainly used CPUs; cloud computing was emerging with CPUs",
        "• Consequence: training ImageNet on CPUs was prohibitively slow",
        "• Does training deep neural networks make sense with your computer?",
        "• Does your computer have less than 40GB of RAM?",
        "• Does your computer have less than 1 TB?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 32,
      "source_text_sha256": "75b303c15607d1974b0937994a7f9028dccc009da91134e5e53198fd5b0cc81c",
      "lines": [
        "(ICML, 2009)",
        "“We argue that modern graphics processors far surpass the computational capabilities of multicore CPUs, and have the",
        "potential to revolutionize the applicability of deep unsupervised learning methods. We develop general principles for",
        "massively parallelizing unsupervised learning tasks using graphics processors… Our implementation of DBN learning is",
        "up to 70 times faster than a dual-core CPU implementation for large models. For example, we are able to reduce the",
        "time required to learn a four-layer DBN with 100 million free parameters from several weeks to around a single day.”",
        "Key Insight:"
      ]
    },
